#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139644 :tutorial/content "To divide square roots, we simply divide the numbers under the root. This is called the **quotient rule for radicals**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt{2} }$ by applying the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt{2} } = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt{5}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule also works for cube roots. For example,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ \\sqrt[3]{10} }{ \\sqrt[3]{2} } = \\sqrt[3]{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt[3]{5}\n$$\n\nIn general, the quotient rule works for any quotient of $n$th roots. So for any $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ \\sqrt[n]{10} }{ \\sqrt[n]{2} } = \\sqrt[n]{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt[n]{5}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** We cannot use the quotient rule when we have the quotient of different radicals, such as square roots and cube roots. For example, the expression $\\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt[3]{5} }$ cannot be simplified because the radicals $\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$ are different." :tutorial/id #uuid "6ea49118-4345-575a-a14f-9aedade211d4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3297 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
